X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:09.800
LLMs are remarkable tools. They can answer questions, summarize and rewrite text, provide feedback on documents, generate code, and much more.

2
00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:18.399
These types of tasks seemed out of reach for computers just a few years ago, and interacting with one can feel a lot like working with another person.

3
00:00:18.399 --> 00:00:27.600
RAG is an approach that further improves the performance of large language models by giving them access to information that they don't know from their training.

4
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:31.500
To help highlight that idea, let's look at a few examples.

5
00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:36.000
Suppose I asked you the question, why are hotels expensive on the weekend?

6
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:42.799
You can probably answer that question. More people travel on weekends, so there's more competition for rooms.

7
00:00:42.799 --> 00:00:49.200
Now, suppose I asked you, why are hotels in Vancouver super expensive this coming weekend?

8
00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:51.700
You'll need more information to answer this one.

9
00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:59.200
If you searched online, you might find that international superstar Taylor Swift is in town this weekend for a two-night residency.

10
00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:04.199
With that additional information, you again probably could answer the question.

11
00:01:04.199 --> 00:01:10.900
Finally, suppose I asked you, why doesn't Vancouver have more hotel capacity close to downtown?

12
00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:20.199
To answer this question, you'd probably need to do some deep research into the history of Vancouver's development, urban planning in general, and so forth.

13
00:01:20.199 --> 00:01:24.500
In other words, you'd need access to a lot of specialized information.

14
00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:29.099
You can think of the way you'd respond to these questions as two phases.

15
00:01:29.099 --> 00:01:37.599
First, you collect any necessary information, and then you reason over that information to develop your response.

16
00:01:37.599 --> 00:01:42.400
As you saw with the first question, sometimes you don't need to collect any information.

17
00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:46.400
Based on your knowledge of the world, you're ready to respond right away.

18
00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:51.599
Other times, though, you might need to collect a little bit or even a lot of information.

19
00:01:51.599 --> 00:01:57.500
In RAG, the process of collecting useful information is called retrieval.

20
00:01:57.500 --> 00:02:03.900
And the process of reasoning over that information and responding is called generation.

21
00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:09.500
LLMs benefit from the retrieval stage for basically the same reasons you do.

22
00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:12.600
You'll learn more about LLMs throughout this course.

23
00:02:12.699 --> 00:02:21.800
But for now, you can think of them as kind of like a person that has a wide variety of general knowledge from reading huge chunks of the Internet.

24
00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:26.000
When you prompt an LLM, it relies on this knowledge to generate a response.

25
00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:28.600
For many prompts, this works great.

26
00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:34.500
In other cases, though, the LLM doesn't know the information it needs to respond accurately.

27
00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:41.699
The prompt might be about a very recent event or some specialized information it hasn't previously seen.

28
00:02:41.699 --> 00:02:47.800
Just as was true with you, it's unreasonable to expect LLMs to be experts on every topic.

29
00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:52.600
And they provide much better responses when they have access to better information.

30
00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:59.500
Recognizing that LLMs also benefit from a retrieval phase is the core idea of RAG.

31
00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:03.100
Of course, LLMs aren't people who spend a lot of time on Wikipedia.

32
00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:10.000
Instead, they're mathematical models that have been trained on massive data sets taken from all over the open Internet.

33
00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:15.600
During the training process, the model learns any information contained in the training data.

34
00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:24.100
When you send a prompt to an LLM, you're hoping information relevant to your question was included in the training data, ideally many times.

35
00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:27.500
Unfortunately, lots of information won't be included.

36
00:03:27.500 --> 00:03:29.800
Companies keep private databases.

37
00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:33.199
Some information is just hidden or hard to access.

38
00:03:33.199 --> 00:03:41.199
And with news being published every minute of the day, there will always be information out there that an LLM wasn't trained on.

39
00:03:41.199 --> 00:03:47.100
The question then is, how do you make sure the LLM knows this useful information?

40
00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:50.100
The short answer, just put it in the prompt.

41
00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:57.899
The key idea of a RAG system is that you can modify a prompt before sending it to the large language model.

42
00:03:57.899 --> 00:04:04.199
In addition to the user's original question, you can add in information that helps the LLM respond.

43
00:04:04.199 --> 00:04:09.399
If you asked a RAG system, why are hotels in Vancouver super expensive this weekend?

44
00:04:09.399 --> 00:04:14.199
It would first perform a retrieval step to gather relevant information.

45
00:04:14.199 --> 00:04:23.000
The language model would then be provided an augmented prompt that includes both the original question and any retrieved information.

46
00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:27.799
Now, the LLM has the information it needs to respond accurately.

47
00:04:27.799 --> 00:04:31.500
Of course, this information needs to be retrieved from somewhere.

48
00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:36.399
The component of a RAG system that handles this process is called a retriever.

49
00:04:36.399 --> 00:04:43.500
The retriever manages a knowledge base of trusted, relevant, and possibly private information.

50
00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:53.000
When the RAG system receives a prompt, the retriever finds and retrieves the most relevant information from the knowledge base to share with the LLM.

51
00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:57.899
The model then uses that retrieved information when it responds to the prompt.

52
00:04:57.899 --> 00:05:06.899
The name Retrieval Augmented Generation, which is admittedly a mouthful, hopefully now makes more sense.

53
00:05:06.899 --> 00:05:17.299
All you're doing is improving or augmenting the way an LLM generates text by first retrieving relevant information from a knowledge base.

54
00:05:17.299 --> 00:05:23.899
This is a high-level description of how RAG works, but sometimes it can be more useful to see some example applications.

55
00:05:23.899 --> 00:05:27.899
So join me in the next video where I'll talk through some ways RAG is already used.

